WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:07.320
The first film traced one direct relay from cytokine receptor to DNA.

2
00:00:08.840 --> 00:00:13.560
How do four JAKs and seven STATs serve dozens of cytokines, keep their signals

3
00:00:13.560 --> 00:00:14.860
apart, and shut off?

4
00:00:16.100 --> 00:00:20.320
The answers sit in two places: the receptor tails and the cell's brakes.

5
00:00:21.433 --> 00:00:25.293
Start at the membrane, where receptor tails do most of the sorting.

6
00:00:26.653 --> 00:00:30.373
Each receptor chain holds a particular JAK near the membrane.

7
00:00:31.753 --> 00:00:35.033
Farther down, its tail carries distinct docking tyrosines.

8
00:00:35.033 --> 00:00:41.913
The amino acids around each phosphotyrosine select which STAT's SH2 domain fits.

9
00:00:43.017 --> 00:00:45.357
Compare two receptors side by side.

10
00:00:46.537 --> 00:00:52.177
The erythropoietin receptor holds JAK2 on its tails to drive red blood cell

11
00:00:52.177 --> 00:00:57.717
production. A different receptor chain in immune cells holds JAK3 instead.

12
00:00:59.097 --> 00:01:01.877
Different tails assign different JAKs to different jobs.

13
00:01:02.950 --> 00:01:05.530
The four JAKs look very similar in sequence.

14
00:01:06.850 --> 00:01:10.170
You might expect them to cover for one another when one is missing.

15
00:01:11.730 --> 00:01:13.930
Suppose a mouse embryo lacks JAK2 completely.

16
00:01:15.290 --> 00:01:19.670
Will the other three JAKs step in for erythropoietin, or will that pathway fail?

17
00:01:25.717 --> 00:01:27.237
That pathway fails.

18
00:01:28.757 --> 00:01:33.557
Without JAK2, mouse embryos make an early wave of red cells, but none of the

19
00:01:33.557 --> 00:01:34.997
lasting, adult kind.

20
00:01:36.337 --> 00:01:40.977
The other three JAKs remain intact, yet on this receptor tail they do not

21
00:01:40.977 --> 00:01:41.977
substitute.

22
00:01:42.650 --> 00:01:45.090
Now ask how a healthy signal turns off.

23
00:01:46.310 --> 00:01:48.290
Two main brakes do most of that work.

24
00:01:49.510 --> 00:01:55.110
First, tyrosine phosphatases strip phosphates from JAKs, receptor tails, and STATs.

25
00:01:56.230 --> 00:02:01.690
Once a STAT loses its phosphate in the nucleus, it returns to the cytoplasm, ready

26
00:02:01.690 --> 00:02:02.690
for reuse.

27
00:02:03.483 --> 00:02:05.923
Second, the pathway makes its own brake.

28
00:02:07.343 --> 00:02:12.503
Among the genes that STATs switch on are the SOCS proteins, suppressors of cytokine

29
00:02:12.503 --> 00:02:18.423
signaling. After a delay, SOCS proteins dock on the active receptor and JAK.

30
00:02:19.343 --> 00:02:23.843
Some block the JAK directly, and several can tag the complex for breakdown.

31
00:02:25.067 --> 00:02:30.567
A third restraint acts before cytokine even arrives: the pseudokinase lobe on each

32
00:02:30.567 --> 00:02:37.727
JAK. In most patients with polycythaemia vera, one amino acid change alters JAK2's

33
00:02:37.727 --> 00:02:42.327
pseudokinase. That weakens restraint and helps two JAKs pair.

34
00:02:43.247 --> 00:02:48.587
JAK2 then fires with little or no erythropoietin, producing too many red cells.

35
00:02:49.600 --> 00:02:55.920
Missing JAK2 silences the erythropoietin receptor; loosening its restraint leaves

36
00:02:55.920 --> 00:02:57.220
the kinase stuck on.

37
00:02:58.540 --> 00:03:03.440
So receptor tails select specific JAKs and STATs, while brakes restrain signaling.

38
00:03:04.760 --> 00:03:10.020
Together, receptor sorting and layered restraint keep a rapid pathway accurate and

39
00:03:10.020 --> 00:03:10.620
under control.
